## Setup

```{r setup}
#| eval: false
install.packages("SHELF")
```

```{r setup-real}
library(tidyverse)
library(SHELF)
library(brms)

theme_set(theme_minimal(base_size = 13))
```

This chapter uses the **`SHELF`** package (the Sheffield Elicitation
Framework, maintained by Jeremy Oakley) — a set of tools built
specifically for turning expert judgement into a fitted probability
distribution, widely used in health economics and risk analysis. It's
a genuinely different tool from anything else in this book: the input
isn't a dataset, it's a conversation.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## The problem elicitation solves

You're about to model the expected effect of a new manager-training
programme on first-year attrition. There's no historical data on *this*
programme yet — but your Head of L&D has run three similar
interventions before and has a real, informed opinion about what to
expect. A default weakly-informative prior throws that opinion away. A
prior "estimated from data" doesn't apply, because there isn't any yet.

::: callout-important
The Head of L&D's judgement **is** data — it's just data in someone's
head instead of a spreadsheet. Elicitation is the structured process
for getting it out in a form a model can use.
:::

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Asking the right questions

Nobody can answer "what's your prior probability distribution for the
effect size?" — that's not how people think. They *can* answer
calibrated questions about specific values:

::: callout-note
## The 10/90 protocol

1.  **"What's a value you're 90% sure the true reduction is *above*?"**
    (a low estimate — the 10th percentile)
2.  **"What's your single best guess?"** (the median)
3.  **"What's a value you're 90% sure the true reduction is *below*?"**
    (a high estimate — the 90th percentile)

Ask for the extremes *before* the middle guess — it reduces the
tendency to anchor tightly around a single number and then treat
everything else as an afterthought. Framing the extremes as
percentiles ("one time in ten, the truth would genuinely surprise
you by falling outside this") also tends to work better than asking
for a "minimum" or "maximum," which people tend to read as hard
bounds rather than honest uncertainty.
:::

Suppose your Head of L&D answers: 1 percentage point (10th
percentile), 5 points (median), 12 points (90th percentile) reduction
in first-year attrition.

### Fitting a distribution to those three numbers

```{r fit-single-expert}
judgements <- fitdist(
  vals  = c(1, 5, 12),
  probs = c(0.1, 0.5, 0.9),
  lower = 0     # a reduction can't be negative in this framing
)

judgements$Normal
judgements$best.fitting
```

`fitdist()` fits several candidate distributions (Normal, log-normal,
gamma, beta, Student-t) to the three elicited points and reports which
fits most closely. Plot it to check the shape makes sense — and, more
importantly, to **show the expert**:

```{r plot-fit}
# plotfit() returns a ggplot object (returnPlot = TRUE; showPlot =
# FALSE stops it printing its own copy first), so we can style it like
# every other chart in this book, and add two reference lines: the
# lower limit we gave fitdist() (solid), and the fitted Normal
# distribution's mean (dashed) - a useful comparison point, since the
# curve on display is the "best" fit (often skew-normal for exactly
# three elicited points) and its mean needn't sit at the same spot.
plotfit(judgements, d = "best", lp = TRUE,
        showPlot = FALSE, returnPlot = TRUE) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = judgements$limits$lower,
             linetype = "solid", colour = "grey40") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = judgements$Normal$mean,
             linetype = "dashed", colour = "#d32f2f") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 13)

# plotfit() also resets ggplot's global default theme as a side effect
# - reset it back so later plots in the book aren't affected.
theme_set(theme_minimal(base_size = 13))
```

```{r feedback}
feedback(judgements, quantiles = c(0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9))
```

::: callout-important
## The feedback loop is the whole point

Show the fitted curve back to the expert and ask: *"does this look
like what you meant?"* Experts are often surprised by how fat or thin
the tails of the fitted distribution are compared to what they
intended — and adjusting the elicited numbers until the fitted curve
actually matches their belief is where the real value of this process
lives. Skipping this step and just taking the first fitted distribution
defeats the purpose.
:::

::: callout-tip
## For the ML/DS crowd: LLMs as a mechanical fitting shortcut

Turning three elicited numbers into distribution parameters is a
solved, mechanical curve-fitting problem — which makes it a
reasonable task to hand to an LLM if you're prototyping and don't
have `SHELF` to hand: describe the elicited 10th/50th/90th
percentiles and ask for the Beta(α, β) or Gamma(α, β) that reproduces
them. It lowers the technical bar without changing anything about the
process itself — you should still verify the distribution you get
back actually reproduces your three numbers, and still show the
fitted curve back to the expert. For real work, `SHELF::fitdist()`
remains the better choice: it fits and compares several candidate
families at once and was purpose-built for exactly this job.
:::

